package dev.apkrelab

/**
 * 0.39.0: what the AI Hub knows about the APP ITSELF (its screens, features, scanner coverage and limits).
 * [overview] is part of the agent's system prompt (short); [topic] is returned by the tool `app_capabilities` (details).
 * KEEP IN SYNC with the code: when a check, tab or feature is added or removed, update the text here too (see HANDOFF.md).
 */
object Capabilities {
    fun overview(): String =
        "APK ReLab is an on-device (Android) STATIC analysis workbench for apps the user owns or may analyze. Screens: Files (APK tree), Manifest (decoded, highlighted, Quick fixes), " +
            "Classes (class index, smali, Java view by jadx, hand editing with diff and revert, global code search), Security (Smart scan, Deep scan, AndroGoat benchmark check, filters, Top 5, Explain exploit, AI triage, reachability from exported components), " +
            "Build (assemble edited smali, apply queued manifest fixes, optional package rename, sign with the user's own key, install / uninstall), AI Hub (this chat: every feature is a tool), Markdown report export, AI settings (providers and keys, team, council, theme, background indexing). " +
            "The scanner is heuristic (regex over smali, manifest and resources): findings are LEADS to verify with get_class / get_java and the call-trace, never proof. " +
            "It cannot run the app (no dynamic analysis, no Frida / traffic capture), does not analyse native .so code, and edits only smali plus boolean manifest flags. " +
            "For details call app_capabilities with topic features | scanner | workflow | limits; never claim a feature or check that is not listed there."

    fun topic(name: String): String = when (name.trim().lowercase()) {
        "features" -> FEATURES
        "scanner", "checks", "coverage" -> SCANNER
        "workflow", "howto" -> WORKFLOW
        "limits", "limitations" -> LIMITS
        else -> "error: topic must be features, scanner, workflow or limits.\n\n" + overview()
    }

    private const val FEATURES = """FEATURES OF APK ReLab
Files: tree of the APK (dex, resources, assets, lib), view text and decoded XML.
Manifest: decoded AndroidManifest.xml with colours for risky flags and permissions; Copy; Review manifest (AI); Quick fixes = queue boolean flips (debuggable, allowBackup, usesCleartextTraffic, exported=false for a component; launcher and deep-link activities are protected) or "Queue all"; queued edits are applied on the next Build.
Classes: class index (background indexing builds a text index of the smali), app/library toggle, code search across ALL classes, class screen: smali view, Java view (jadx, read-only, approximate), Edit (save, diff, revert), Suggest (AI edit with diff and Apply), Explain (AI), Methods, Rename ideas (AI, read-only), Patterns (AI), Security review, Fix vulnerability.
Security: Smart scan = manifest + Deep scan (code) + resource scan + AI triage + call graph; Deep scan alone; AndroGoat check (benchmark on the AndroGoat test app); severity chips (CRIT/HIGH/MED/LOW/INFO), scope filter (APP / LIB / OBF), Top 5 confirmed findings, Explain exploit (idea, impact, safe check, fix), reachability from exported components with the call path.
Build: assemble edited smali, patch the manifest flips, optional package rename (so the modified app installs next to the original), zip-align and sign with the user's own key (key can be exported / imported), install, uninstall the original, build log.
AI Hub: this chat. Tools are grouped Inspect, Security, AI analysis, Edit, Build and install, Report. A lead model plans; worker models can analyse classes in parallel (delegate / delegate_many); a council (critic and judge on other models) reviews edits and confirmed findings. The user can attach images, PDFs and text files to a message. Map tab shows the tools being used live.
Report: Markdown or HTML export (app info, manifest, edit diffs, AI notes, security findings as educational cards: why flagged, risk scenario, fix, OWASP Mobile Top 10 / MASVS / CWE and a remediation guide with vulnerable vs secure code). The finding dialog shows the same card (confidence, scenario, code samples, reference links).
Settings: AI providers and keys (Gemini first, optional Groq / OpenRouter), lead model, delegation switch, council switch, background indexing switch, theme (Midnight, Ocean, Night)."""

    private const val SCANNER = """WHAT THE SECURITY SCANNER CHECKS (heuristic; a finding is a lead, not proof)
MANIFEST: debuggable; testOnly; allowBackup; cleartext traffic allowed; no network security config; exported activity / service / receiver / provider without a permission; exported component guarded by a weak custom permission (normal / dangerous); provider that protects only reads or only writes; provider with broad grantUriPermissions; task hijacking (taskAffinity + singleTask / singleInstance); deep-link entry points (custom scheme without host, links without autoVerify); powerful and sensitive permissions; low minSdkVersion; old targetSdkVersion; sharedUserId; requestLegacyExternalStorage; accessibility service declared.
CODE (smali): WebView (addJavascriptInterface, file access from file URLs, mixed content, remote debugging, SSL errors ignored, dynamic content loaded, URL taken straight from an Intent, JavaScript enabled); TLS (no-op TrustManager, hostname verification disabled, insecure SSL socket factory, SSL / TLS 1.0-1.1 requested); crypto (ECB or bare AES / DES, legacy ciphers, RSA PKCS#1 padding, MD5 / SHA-1, hardcoded key, static IV, PBKDF2 with low iterations, SecureRandom SHA1PRNG seeded manually, non-crypto Random near security code, JWT signing secret hardcoded); hardcoded secrets (known key formats such as AWS, Stripe, GitHub, Slack, Telegram, JWT, SendGrid, GitLab, FCM, Mapbox, Shopify, Square, Google OAuth, Twilio, npm, Discord and Slack webhooks, HTTP Basic credentials, private keys, Google API keys, cloud storage and Firebase URLs; credential-named fields; high-entropy strings near credential names); storage (world-readable modes, SharedPreferences / SQLite with sensitive values, temp files, shared external storage, clipboard); IPC (PendingIntent without FLAG_IMMUTABLE, dynamic receiver without export flag, Intent.parseUri, nested Intent from extras launched = intent redirection, PreferenceActivity.isValidFragment always true = fragment injection, ContentProvider.query SQL built by concatenation, openFile without canonicalisation); injection and execution (SQL built by string concatenation or from UI input, shell command execution, dynamic code loading, Java deserialization, Zip Slip); logging (sensitive data in logs, OkHttp body / header logging); other (cleartext URLs, biometric prompt without CryptoObject, HTTP client without pinning, QR input reaching a sink, Firebase database SDK / URL, INFO-level root / emulator / signature-check detection).
RESOURCES (13 rules res-*, ScanRulesResources.kt): network security config (cleartext permitted for every domain or for named domains, user CAs trusted in base / domain / debug-only, overridePins, no pin-set, pin-set expired / expiring / no backup pin); strings (read from the resources.arsc string pool, or from plain res/values files in unpacked projects): Google API key, Firebase Realtime Database URL, AWS access key (pair), JWT (judged by its claims: service_role, anon, expired), other known token formats (Anthropic, OpenAI, Hugging Face, GitHub, GitLab, Slack, Stripe, SendGrid, Telegram, FCM legacy, npm, Shopify, Square, Azure, Google OAuth secret, private key PEM, webhooks, bucket URLs), credentials inside a URL, credential-named strings with a random-looking value (plain values files only: names are not in the arsc pool); sensitive EditText without a no-suggestions input type (keyboard cache); FileProvider paths that share a whole tree.
TAINT ANALYSIS (0.44.0, rule taint-flow): follows untrusted input from its source to a dangerous API inside ONE class. Sources: Intent extras / data, parameters of @JavascriptInterface methods, parameters of ContentProvider methods. Sinks: WebView loadUrl / loadData / evaluateJavascript, SQL (rawQuery, execSQL, query selection / orderBy, delete / update where), Runtime.exec / ProcessBuilder, file streams and File.delete (path traversal), Class.forName / getMethod, DexClassLoader / System.load, URL / OkHttp url (SSRF). The value is followed through registers, String / StringBuilder / Uri / File / Bundle / JSON / collection helpers, fields of the class and calls between methods of the class; sanitizers (URLEncoder, sqlEscapeString, parseInt ...) stop it. Confidence HIGH = proven chain with no validation-like call in the method, MEDIUM = passed through unknown app code or a validation-like call exists, LOW = both. CROSS-CLASS (0.49.0, rule taint-xclass): every method of the app's own classes gets a small summary (which parameter reaches which sink, which calls receive a tainted value, what it returns); after the scan the summaries are chained up to 4 calls deep, so an Intent value passed to a helper class that loads it in a WebView / opens it as a file is reported at the sink. Confidence is at most MEDIUM; calls are matched by the class named in the invoke (virtual / interface dispatch, lambdas, reflection, fields of other classes are not followed). It has no path sensitivity: always check that the component is exported / reachable and read the code. SSA TAINT (0.56.0, same rule id taint-flow, the finding text says SSA data-flow engine): after the class scan, a second engine (SSA form over a call graph of the whole app) adds the flows the in-class engine did not report, across methods and classes, keeping reused registers apart; it only adds a finding when the class has no taint-flow finding with that title yet, and it also reports flows in classes no exported component reaches (reach NO, confidence one step lower).
LIBRARIES / SCA (0.45.0, rules sca-known-vuln, sca-unmaintained, sca-inventory in ScanLibraries.kt): finds third-party libraries inside the BUILT apk and compares them offline with a small hand-checked table of known vulnerable versions (Log4j, commons-text, OkHttp, Gson, Jackson, Retrofit, Netty, Play Core, BouncyCastle, OpenSSL, curl, zlib, libpng). Versions come from META-INF/*.version files, pom.properties, version strings inside lib/<abi>/*.so and the okhttp User-Agent literal in classes.dex. Lists the libraries as an SBOM (CycloneDX 1.5 in the Markdown report) and flags unmaintained ones (android.support, Play Core). It does NOT check whether the app reaches the vulnerable code, and most plain Java jars leave no version after R8, so an empty list is not proof that the app is clean. 0.48.0 adds ten OVAA-driven code rules: unprotected broadcast with credentials, sensitive implicit Intent launch, Serializable/Parcelable classes that delete files or declare native methods, provider openFile built from the URI, code loaded through createPackageContext, dex loaded from a shared/world-accessible directory, ACTION_PICK result trusted, weak deep link host check (endsWith/contains), deep link parameter saved as configuration, and URI-permission grant returned through setResult. All are single-method heuristics (no data-flow between methods or classes). R8 / ProGuard shrinking renames packages (okhttp3 becomes a.b.c), so the package-path layer works best on debug or unshrunk APKs; on a minified release APK treat the library list as a lower bound and keep your confidence low. The vulnerability table is read from assets/vulns.json (built-in copy as fallback); entries marked "advisory" are general notices, not CVEs.
FINTECH (0.52.0, rules fintech-* in ScanRulesFinTech.kt, aimed at trading/banking/payment apps): secrets/tokens that cross an RxJava / Kotlin Coroutine / AsyncTask boundary (live price feeds, order submission, token refresh) before reaching a network or log sink (fintech-async-taint); PIN / API key / session token written to plain SharedPreferences with no EncryptedSharedPreferences or Keystore use anywhere in the class (fintech-unencrypted-storage); hardcoded ws:// (vs wss://) endpoints and OkHttp WebSocket handshakes opened with no Authorization/token header nearby - the channel most trading apps use for live market data (fintech-insecure-websocket); an Activity/Service/BroadcastReceiver that reads an Intent extra straight into a method whose name looks like it moves money (trade/order/withdraw/transfer/deposit/payout/payment/sendMoney ...) with no checkCallingPermission / checkSelfPermission / getCallingUid / biometric re-auth call in between - cross-check android:exported in the manifest before treating it as reachable (fintech-exported-sensitive-action); client-side Frida / Xposed / instrumentation-detection markers, reported INFO as a threat-model-maturity signal alongside the existing root/emulator checks (fintech-frida-detection). These are heuristic, single-class checks like the rest of the scanner - verify reachability and exported status before reporting any of them as confirmed.
IR RULES (0.75.0, ScanRulesIr.kt + ir/IrRules.java, decided on the SSA of one class): broadcast-unprotected-send / implicit-service-intent (the Intent is traced back to its `new Intent`; flagged only if no setPackage / setComponent / setClass(Name), no receiver permission and the Intent never leaves the method; severity from the extras), sqlite-unencrypted (a sensitive column name or a PII source such as AccountManager.getPassword / getDeviceId / Location reaches ContentValues.put / execSQL / SQLiteStatement.bind in plain SQLite with no hash or cipher on the way; otherwise one INFO per SQLiteOpenHelper; SQLCipher classes = quiet), webview-plugin-state (setPluginState with PluginState.ON / ON_DEMAND traced through registers and phis, setPluginsEnabled(true)), tapjacking-sensitive-layout (res layout with a password field and no filterTouchesWhenObscured, cross-checked with mitigation APIs in the dex and minSdk).
ARCHITECTURE (0.44.0): every code check, every manifest check AND every resource check is a plugin rule (RuleRegistry: 13 resource rules with ids res-*, in ScanRulesResources.kt, run once per resource file with a light ResourceContext; 18 manifest rules with ids manifest-*, in ScanRulesManifest.kt, run once on the parsed manifest; 47 code rules: taint-flow plus WebView, crypto, TLS, secrets, storage, IPC, SQL, logs, the five fintech-* rules ... plus the AndroGoat checks and the entropy scan); each finding records the rule id, a CWE with an educational card (VulnKnowledge) and a confidence. A rule that fails is skipped and counted, never fatal. Added in 0.40.0: hardware identifier read - IMEI / IMSI / phone number (LOW), RSA / DSA / DH key shorter than 2048 bits (MEDIUM). False-positive reduction (FindingPolish): findings in test classes become INFO; heuristic findings inside known library code are lowered one level (reason stored in the finding); each finding has a confidence (HIGH = exact API or manifest flag, MEDIUM = heuristic, LOW = informational). Not applicable on Android: CSRF (no browser session cookies in native code); SQL injection, XSS in WebView, insecure deserialization, hardcoded secrets and broken access control (exported components) were already covered.
AFTER THE SCAN: each finding has a severity, CWE, scope (APP / LIB / OBF), AI verdict (TP / FP / REVIEW) and, when traced, a reachability path from an exported component. LIB findings are usually not the app's own code.
NOISE REDUCTION (0.46.0): INFO findings are hidden by default (severity chips or Filter: ALL reveal them); repeated findings are merged into one xN card (same rule+class, and INFO across classes); heuristic findings in obfuscated packages are lowered one level and hidden with the Libs filter, while CRITICAL/HIGH hits in obfuscated classes stay visible. resources.arsc strings are mapped to their entry names (api_key, auth_token ...) so name-based secret rules work on the built APK. Taint sources: Intent extras, JavascriptInterface and ContentProvider parameters, SharedPreferences reads. The report carries an Attack surface table (exported components with their intent-filter actions, requested permissions)."""

    private const val WORKFLOW = """HOW TO FIND VULNERABILITIES WITH THIS APP
1. app_info, then scan_security smart (can take minutes; background indexing helps). Then audit_brief: one deterministic page (counts, the reachable app-code findings, what the scan could not see) to plan the review. 2. get_findings: start with CRITICAL / HIGH in scope APP that are reachable (reach YES) or have a TP verdict.
3. VERIFY each candidate, in this order: flow_path #N (the entry chain and the data path); for every renamed class on that path, classify_class (an R8-merged class or a static holder can join unrelated code: a flow through a merged class's shared field is a lead until the smali shows the writer and the reader are the same original class); get_class (exact smali) or get_java around the source and the sink; entry_points / search_code for the callers and any check or sanitiser. Do not report an unverified finding as confirmed.
3b. Before proposing a code fix, run verify_class_edit with the full patched class: it must assemble and the finding must appear under "Removed by the edit" with nothing under "NEW". Only then propose_edit.
4. For attacker-controlled sinks (WebView, SQL, file paths, Intent redirection, deserialization) trace the entry point: exported component -> method -> sink.
5. Explain impact in plain words and give a safe way to check it on the user's own device, then the fix. 6. Manifest problems: queue_manifest_fix or queue_manifest_all, then build_apk, install_apk, and re-scan to confirm the finding is gone. 7. Code problems: propose_edit (the user sees a diff), then build.
Secrets: a found key must be treated as leaked (rotate it). Reviews of one class: security_review, or delegate to a worker with role security."""

    private const val LIMITS = """LIMITS (be honest about them)
Library check (SCA) is a lower bound with a small hand-made vulnerability table, not a full CVE database; "vulnerable version" does not mean "exploitable here". Taint analysis stops at the class boundary (a source in one class and the sink in another is not connected) and ignores branches, lambdas and reflection. Static analysis only: the app is never run, so runtime behaviour, server-side bugs, real network traffic, native (.so) code, obfuscated strings built at runtime and logic flaws are mostly invisible.
Heuristics: regex over smali / manifest / resources. False positives (library code, dead code, constants) and false negatives (values built dynamically, split strings, reflection) both happen; LIB scope findings are usually not the app's own bug.
Edits: smali edits per class and boolean manifest flips only (no new permissions, no resource editing, no intent-filter changes, the launcher must stay exported). Split APKs: only the base APK is patched. DEX version 040 and newer is untested.
Java view: jadx per class, read-only, approximate; never used for edits or for scanning.
AI: models can be wrong; worker answers are unverified until checked against the real code; free-tier quotas may make answers slow or fall back to another model.
Scope: do not help bypass licensing, DRM, payments or protections, or redistribute modified third-party apps."""
}
